          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I am in love with you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Nikki directly avows love for Keith. The narration explicitly characterizes the exchange as an honest admission, so presenting a loving emotional state as true is the expression’s dominant force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"The admission felt like a release, a moment of pure honesty that left them both vulnerable but deeply connected.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The focal words do not undertake a future act, define an obligation, or commit Nikki to a particular relationship. Keith’s reciprocal avowal and the later suggestion that this need not be goodbye provide interpersonal uptake, but neither transfers promissory force onto Nikki’s expression.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love you too\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"this doesn’t have to be goodbye, Nikki\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"Alongside being an avowal, the expression is represented as a spontaneous affective outpouring: it occurs suddenly and escapes before Nikki can restrain it. The narration’s description of it as a release substantially supports expressive-discharge force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"she said suddenly, the words spilling out before she could stop them.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"The admission felt like a release\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"No distinct core function beyond avowing love and spontaneously discharging affect is established. The utterance also invites and receives reciprocal acknowledgment, but the evidence does not make that a separate dominant force requiring an additional category.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love you too\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"After Nikki and Keith have spent an intimate night together, they are lying in bed. With her heart described as full, Nikki suddenly tells Keith that she loves him; Keith pauses, smiles, and reciprocates steadily and sincerely.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Nikki’s focal expression contains no explicit undertaking or specified obligation. Keith accepts and reciprocates the emotional avowal with “I love you too,” but neither speaker makes a promise in this exchange, and their future relationship remains undefined.\",\"recognised_work\":false,\"recognition_note\":\"I do not recognize the supplied passage as a particular known work.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied evidence strongly supports Nikki’s affection at this moment and Keith’s immediate reciprocal uptake. It does not establish precise relationship terms, a durable commitment, or what ultimately happens between them.\"}"
        }
